    "font" : "Arial",
    "font size" : 9.7,
    "text color" : "[0.0]",
    "content" : "In April of 1987, the study was expanded by adding gypsum as a third soil amendment treatment (see Schuman et al 1989 for full detail). Two gypsum regimes were superimposed by dividing each native species plot, and randomly designating each portion to receive gypsum at either O or 56 Mg/ha. The 56 Mg/ha gypsum rate supplied sufficient Ca ions to reduce the exchangeable sodium percentage (ESP) of the spoil to 15. The gypsum was surface-applied with no subsequent soil incorporation, to avoid physically disrupting previously established vegetation."
